like that also  when you i just want to  stress and underline this because here  again i completely agree  and the problem is that in most cases  monday morning you will need to wake up  really early to get one of the earlier  flights so depending a bit on your  location the connection  often you will need to wake up at 5am  sometimes even earlier  so this means you already start your  work week with some kind of sleep  deficit  and then this just continues building  over the work week and then usually on  thursday evening  or then when the week is over you really  need to do lots  of sleeping on the weekend to recover of  course now a bit depending on your  individual sleep needs but this then  leads to really don't having that much  time also not on the weekends  and this is of course why this travel  can be quite a burden and adds to the  not so ideal work-life balance for many  projects when you travel a lot for work  your work time and personal time tend to  blend together a lot for example  sometimes i need to book a flight that  doesn't get me home  until 11 30 at night which means a lot  of my personal time has been taken up by  work pro number four  is that you get to work with a lot of  big companies often fortune 100  companies that  are really cool and well-known that  means you might get to go to the  headquarters of a really well-known  company meet with  leaders there and it's just really cool  that you get to help  shape companies that are so well known  across the world  i agree with that this is indeed what  you will frequently do  and of course you also depends a bit on  your taste so for me personally  one of the best experiences i had was  rather working for  some more medium sized companies in  consulting not necessarily the very big  corporates  and the main reason being that there  sometimes at least i  felt like the possibilities that you  have the things that you can really  change  the time to impact the path to impact  was even shorter was even more direct  but now this might also be a question of  personal preference  so yes of course you will work with very  large clients and you will meet lots of  senior executives this way  con is that those companies and clients  could be a lot more demanding this is  really a generalization because some  people  and clients you'll just get along with  better on a personal level  just because your working styles or your  personalities match so regardless of the  company there are going to be some  clients  that are more demanding or more  difficult to work with and others that  are a little easier to work with but i  would say that the bigger the client or  more well-known the client  is there's just that much more pressure  to make sure you're performing and  bringing results and pro  so here i'd say i didn't see that  correlation that strongly right i had  great clients to work with at very large  companies that  are super difficult people to work with  at smaller companies  but overall i agree that you know the  type of clients you have or you can work  together with them  really shapes your experience and it can  be really hard as a consultant  if you just don't get along well with  your clients so this is for sure a very  decisive factor pro number five  is that the compensation or the pay is  usually pretty good  according to glassdoor the average  salary for a consultant in the us  is about 79 000 which when you compare  to a salary for  a software engineer for example is  actually kind of low but when you look  at the median salary in the us  as a whole which is 61 000 then it's  actually on the more highly compensated  side the con  is that while you might earn more money  than you would in some other careers  because of some of the challenges i  mentioned earlier in the video  like demanding work schedules and  difficulty to achieve work-life balance  a lot of consultants tend to get burned  out and leave after just a few years for  example  so here just to react to that so on the  salary side of course she is right yes  consulting in general is a  good and rather high paying career for  many of them and then it's also no  secret that the mbb consulting firms  are one of the firms that pay the  highest salaries right so you can expect  yourself being even at the  higher end of all the spectrum so yes of  course the salaries are high  but what she also rightfully explains is  that the demands are higher on you as  well  so you will have a less well work life  balance there will be quite a  significant amount of stress and  pressure on you  and yes some people do burn out and do  struggle  and it is also correct that after some  years many leave the consulting firm  though i would argue that this is not  only due to burnout in the work life  pressure  but also just for other career related  choices as well well at bcg  43 of the employees have been there for  less than two years and 77 have been  there for less than five years so  despite the pay  because of the lifestyle of consulting a  lot of consultants tend to leave after  just a few years now as a last pro  and flip side to that many of these  consultants leave their jobs because  they have an even better opportunity  whether it's a job at a different  company  or maybe it's an acceptance to a  prestigious business school whatever the  reason  having that consulting experience on  your resume makes you a very valuable  asset which is why so many people decide  to go into consulting in the first place  so yes agree with that consulting is a  great career choice and also does open  many doors of you  and especially now with an mbb  background these are of course kind of  household name consulting brands  and there are different firms that  really specifically look  out for these profiles so then higher so  yes if you have this background  it is a great career choice it can open  many doors for you  of course there are other choices other  paths you can go  that might provide you with great  opportunities as well but of course  at least from my experience what i have  seen this is something that might be a  path very worth going that's it guys i  hope you took some valuable information  out of there and if you have any  questions just leave me a comment below  in the comment section and i will do my  very best to answer  every single one if you took any value  out of this video please destroy the  like button for the youtube algorithm  and if you want to see more content like  that subscribe to my channel i'm  uploading at least  one new video every saturday on my  channel i talk about  my experiences being a former mckinsey  strategy consultant and i want to help  you to become successful in the first  years of your career and if you want to  see even more from me i also have an  instagram channel  my handle is firm learning and i also  have an email mailing list you can sign  up for that for even more content the  sign up link is below in the video  description  thank you guys for watching hope you're  all having a great day and see you next  saturday
 

